WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.380
NARRATOR: Data management begins with a model,
not a query.

2
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.568
NARRATOR: A relation is commonly presented as
a table.

3
00:00:06.568 --> 00:00:12.731
NARRATOR: Columns represent attributes with
defined domains, and rows represent tuples

4
00:00:12.731 --> 00:00:13.852
describing instances.

5
00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:19.417
NARRATOR: A students table might contain a
student identifier and name; an enrollments

6
00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:23.698
table can represent the relationship between
students and course sections.

7
00:00:23.698 --> 00:00:29.132
NARRATOR: Every column needs one meaning, one
unit, and an appropriate type.

8
00:00:29.132 --> 00:00:34.515
NARRATOR: Avoid storing several values in one
cell or repeating groups of columns.

9
00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:39.056
NARRATOR: A schema is a contract about the
shape and interpretation of stored data.

10
00:00:39.056 --> 00:00:44.172
NARRATOR: If the meaning is ambiguous,
technically valid SQL can still produce

11
00:00:44.172 --> 00:00:45.025
meaningless results.

12
00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:50.872
NARRATOR: A key is an attribute or minimal set
of attributes that uniquely identifies a row.

13
00:00:50.872 --> 00:00:56.561
NARRATOR: A table may have several candidate
keys; one is selected as the primary key.

14
00:00:56.561 --> 00:01:02.282
NARRATOR: Stable generated identifiers are
often useful, but a generated key does not

15
00:01:02.282 --> 00:01:06.242
remove the need to enforce real-world
uniqueness when required.

16
00:01:06.242 --> 00:01:12.500
NARRATOR: A foreign key stores a value that
must match a referenced key, representing a

17
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.334
declared relationship.

18
00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:17.173
NARRATOR: An enrollment can reference one
student and one section.

19
00:01:17.173 --> 00:01:21.504
NARRATOR: Foreign-key constraints prevent
orphaned references, but designers must choose

20
00:01:21.504 --> 00:01:25.834
what should happen when referenced data
changes or is deleted.

21
00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:31.548
NARRATOR: Keys give relationships executable
structure rather than leaving them as comments

22
00:01:31.548 --> 00:01:32.977
or naming conventions.

23
00:01:33.098 --> 00:01:36.759
NARRATOR: Types define which values a column
can represent.

24
00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:41.479
NARRATOR: Constraints narrow those values to
the valid states of the application.

25
00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:43.634
NARRATOR: Not null requires a value.

26
00:01:43.634 --> 00:01:47.716
NARRATOR: Check constraints express conditions
such as a nonnegative quantity.

27
00:01:47.716 --> 00:01:50.484
NARRATOR: Unique constraints prevent
duplicates.

28
00:01:50.484 --> 00:01:54.808
NARRATOR: Primary and foreign keys enforce
identity and references.

29
00:01:54.808 --> 00:01:59.765
NARRATOR: Put durable invariants in the
database when every application must obey

30
00:01:59.765 --> 00:02:00.178
them.

31
00:02:00.178 --> 00:02:05.763
NARRATOR: Validation in a user interface
improves feedback, but it can be bypassed by

32
00:02:05.763 --> 00:02:07.359
another client or import.

33
00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:12.914
NARRATOR: Constraints also need careful scope:
a rule involving current time or external

34
00:02:12.914 --> 00:02:16.759
state may not behave like a stable row
invariant.

35
00:02:16.759 --> 00:02:22.027
NARRATOR: Good constraints make invalid data
difficult to store and failures explicit.

36
00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:27.845
NARRATOR: A basic select statement identifies
source tables, filters rows with a where

37
00:02:27.845 --> 00:02:31.785
condition, and projects the columns or
expressions to return.

38
00:02:31.785 --> 00:02:35.126
NARRATOR: The result is itself a table-like
relation.

39
00:02:35.126 --> 00:02:40.301
NARRATOR: SQL describes the desired result
more than a step-by-step loop, allowing the

40
00:02:40.301 --> 00:02:42.690
database to choose an execution plan.

41
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:48.090
NARRATOR: Be precise about null: it represents
missing or unknown information and does not

42
00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.790
behave like an ordinary value in comparisons.

43
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.838
NARRATOR: Use IS NULL rather than equals null.

44
00:02:53.838 --> 00:02:58.073
NARRATOR: Ordering is not guaranteed unless an
ORDER BY clause requests it.

45
00:02:58.073 --> 00:03:04.778
NARRATOR: Select only needed columns, use
meaningful aliases, and verify the result on a

46
00:03:04.778 --> 00:03:08.609
tiny hand-checkable dataset before trusting a
large output.

47
00:03:08.738 --> 00:03:12.680
NARRATOR: Joins reconstruct relationships
stored across tables.

48
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:17.246
NARRATOR: An inner join keeps combinations
that satisfy the join condition.

49
00:03:17.246 --> 00:03:22.575
NARRATOR: A left outer join also keeps
unmatched rows from the left side, filling

50
00:03:22.575 --> 00:03:24.478
missing right-side values with nulls.

51
00:03:24.478 --> 00:03:30.576
NARRATOR: Always identify the intended
cardinality: one to one, one to many, or many

52
00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:33.190
to many through an associative table.

53
00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:38.232
NARRATOR: A missing predicate creates a
Cartesian product, while an incomplete

54
00:03:38.232 --> 00:03:40.524
predicate can silently duplicate results.

55
00:03:40.524 --> 00:03:45.830
NARRATOR: Qualify ambiguous column names and
inspect row counts before and after the join.

56
00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:48.254
NARRATOR: Ask what one result row represents.

57
00:03:48.254 --> 00:03:53.248
NARRATOR: If that sentence is unclear,
aggregation and reporting built on the join

58
00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:54.784
will also be unclear.

59
00:03:54.914 --> 00:04:00.004
NARRATOR: Count, sum, average, minimum, and
maximum summarize rows.

60
00:04:00.004 --> 00:04:03.358
NARRATOR: Group by determines which rows share
one output group.

61
00:04:03.358 --> 00:04:08.269
NARRATOR: If a query groups by course, each
result row represents one course.

62
00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:13.409
NARRATOR: Columns in the select list must
either define that grain or be aggregated.

63
00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:19.384
NARRATOR: Where filters individual rows before
grouping; having filters groups after

64
00:04:19.384 --> 00:04:19.927
aggregation.

65
00:04:19.927 --> 00:04:24.341
NARRATOR: Null handling matters because many
aggregates ignore null values.

66
00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:29.934
NARRATOR: Distinct may remove duplicates, but
using it to hide a faulty join masks the real

67
00:04:29.934 --> 00:04:30.284
problem.

68
00:04:30.284 --> 00:04:36.285
NARRATOR: State the result grain in words,
create a small example, and verify that every

69
00:04:36.285 --> 00:04:39.085
join and group preserves the intended meaning.

70
00:04:39.218 --> 00:04:44.856
NARRATOR: A poorly designed table may repeat
the same course title, instructor office, or

71
00:04:44.856 --> 00:04:47.675
department name across hundreds of enrollment
rows.

72
00:04:47.675 --> 00:04:52.125
NARRATOR: Repetition creates anomalies: one
update can leave conflicting versions,

73
00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:57.910
deleting the last enrollment can erase course
information, and inserting a course may

74
00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.690
require a fake enrollment.

75
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.663
NARRATOR: Normalization analyzes which
attributes depend on which keys and separates

76
00:05:04.663 --> 00:05:07.828
different kinds of facts into related tables.

77
00:05:07.828 --> 00:05:13.118
NARRATOR: Decomposition should preserve the
ability to reconstruct necessary information

78
00:05:13.118 --> 00:05:16.821
through joins and enforce dependencies where
possible.

79
00:05:16.821 --> 00:05:21.157
NARRATOR: Normalization is not a command to
maximize table count.

80
00:05:21.157 --> 00:05:26.729
NARRATOR: It is a reasoning tool for storing
each durable fact in a controlled place while

81
00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:28.122
keeping the model usable.

82
00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.902
NARRATOR: An index is an auxiliary structure
that helps the database locate rows without

83
00:05:33.902 --> 00:05:35.113
scanning every row.

84
00:05:35.113 --> 00:05:40.608
NARRATOR: A B-tree index often supports
equality and range conditions on its leading

85
00:05:40.608 --> 00:05:41.031
keys.

86
00:05:41.031 --> 00:05:44.373
NARRATOR: Unique indexes can also enforce
uniqueness.

87
00:05:44.373 --> 00:05:46.057
NARRATOR: Indexes are not free.

88
00:05:46.057 --> 00:05:50.962
NARRATOR: Inserts, updates, and deletes must
maintain them, they consume storage, and an

89
00:05:50.962 --> 00:05:55.113
index that does not match query predicates may
not be used.

90
00:05:55.113 --> 00:06:00.687
NARRATOR: The query planner estimates costs
and chooses among scans and join strategies.

91
00:06:00.687 --> 00:06:06.022
NARRATOR: Index columns based on important
workload patterns, then inspect actual plans

92
00:06:06.022 --> 00:06:06.911
and measurements.

93
00:06:06.911 --> 00:06:12.027
NARRATOR: Adding an index to every column can
make writes slower without improving the

94
00:06:12.027 --> 00:06:13.123
queries that matter.

95
00:06:13.274 --> 00:06:18.887
NARRATOR: Many business actions require
several statements to succeed as one unit.

96
00:06:18.887 --> 00:06:23.058
NARRATOR: A transfer may subtract from one
account and add to another.

97
00:06:23.058 --> 00:06:28.229
NARRATOR: If the process fails between those
statements, the database must not preserve

98
00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:29.422
half a transfer.

99
00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:35.011
NARRATOR: A transaction begins a unit of work
and ends with commit to make changes durable

100
00:06:35.011 --> 00:06:36.757
or rollback to discard them.

101
00:06:36.757 --> 00:06:42.696
NARRATOR: Concurrent transactions also need
isolation rules so interleaving operations do

102
00:06:42.696 --> 00:06:44.856
not produce invalid results.

103
00:06:44.856 --> 00:06:50.564
NARRATOR: Keep transactions as short as
practical, handle failures explicitly, and do

104
00:06:50.564 --> 00:06:56.272
not assume that application code alone can
restore consistency after every crash.

105
00:06:56.272 --> 00:07:01.923
NARRATOR: Transaction boundaries should match
the atomic action users and business rules

106
00:07:01.923 --> 00:07:02.394
recognize.

107
00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:06.515
NARRATOR: A professional database design
answers nine questions.

108
00:07:06.515 --> 00:07:08.938
NARRATOR: What does each table and row
represent?

109
00:07:08.938 --> 00:07:12.637
NARRATOR: Which keys identify rows and connect
relations?

110
00:07:12.637 --> 00:07:15.239
NARRATOR: Which constraints prevent invalid
states?

111
00:07:15.239 --> 00:07:18.097
NARRATOR: What is the grain of each query
result?

112
00:07:18.097 --> 00:07:21.744
NARRATOR: Do join predicates match the
intended cardinality?

113
00:07:21.744 --> 00:07:24.589
NARRATOR: Are repeated facts creating
anomalies?

114
00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:27.484
NARRATOR: Which workload justifies each index?

115
00:07:27.484 --> 00:07:29.780
NARRATOR: Which changes must commit
atomically?

116
00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.364
NARRATOR: What do query plans and measurements
show on realistic data?

117
00:07:33.364 --> 00:07:36.489
NARRATOR: SQL syntax is only the surface.

118
00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:42.173
NARRATOR: Trustworthy data systems come from
preserving meaning and integrity while giving

119
00:07:42.173 --> 00:07:46.910
applications efficient, testable ways to
derive results and apply changes.

120
00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:52.366
NARRATOR: Finish by testing the schema with a
tiny dataset that contains a valid

121
00:07:52.366 --> 00:07:57.432
relationship, a missing reference, a
duplicate, a null, and a transaction that must

122
00:07:57.432 --> 00:07:58.211
roll back.

123
00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:02.000
NARRATOR: Then inspect the actual query plan
for the most important read.

124
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.733
NARRATOR: Those checks connect the conceptual
model to enforceable behavior and measured

125
00:08:07.733 --> 00:08:08.211
performance.
